---
import Article from "../components/article.astro";
import { Definition } from "../components/definition";
import Heading from "../components/heading.astro";
import { ClassifyFreeAndBoundMorphemes } from "../components/morphemes/classify-free-and-bound-morphemes";
import { SeparateUnbreakable } from "../components/morphemes/separate-unbreakable";
import Paragraph from "../components/paragraph.astro";
import { Popup } from "../components/popup";
import { WaitUntil } from "../components/wait-until";
import SectionLayout from "../layouts/section.astro";
---

<SectionLayout
  title="Morphemes"
  position="2"
  previousPath="/morphology"
  nextPath="/inflection-and-derivation"
>
  <Article>
    <Heading>Morphemes</Heading>

    <Paragraph>
      At the very core of morphology lies the concept of a
      <b>morpheme</b>.
    </Paragraph>

    <Definition
      term="morpheme"
      category="noun"
      ipa="mɔːrfiːm"
      definition="the smallest unit of language that contains meaning"
    />

    <Paragraph>
      Usually, this refers to the smaller components within a word that
      contribute to its meaning. In the previous “splinched” example, both
      “splinch” and “-ed” are individual morphemes that come together to form
      the past tense of “splinch.”
    </Paragraph>

    <SeparateUnbreakable client:idle />

    <WaitUntil client:idle page="morphemes" progress={1}>
      <Paragraph> Looks like that wasn’t too impossible… </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        Morphemes can also be independent words themselves. Take the morpheme
        “break” for example—it can stand on its own, as well as join other
        morphemes to form a larger word. Such morphemes are called
        <b>free morphemes</b>.
      </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        The opposite of a free morpheme is a <b>bound morpheme</b>, which can
        only be part of a larger word and cannot stand on its own. In the
        example above, the prefix “un-” is a bound morpheme because it needs to
        attach to a base to modify its meaning, and it is not a valid word on
        its own.
      </Paragraph>

      <ClassifyFreeAndBoundMorphemes client:idle />
    </WaitUntil>

    <WaitUntil client:idle page="morphemes" progress={2}>
      <Paragraph>Great work!</Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        Another way to classify morphemes is by their semantic attributes. In
        English words with

        <Popup client:idle triggerText="multiple morphemes">
          <p>
            Such words are known as <b>polymorphemic words</b> (like <i
              >unbreakable</i
            >), which have two or more morphemes that fuse together to form the
            word.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is in contrast to <b>monomorphemic words</b>, which are made up
            of only one morpheme (like <i>break</i>).
          </p>
        </Popup>, we usually observe a
        <b>base</b> that contains the core meaning of the word, and
        <b>affixes</b> that attach to the base to modify its meaning.
      </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        In the “unbreakable” example, the morpheme “break” is a base, whereas
        “un-” and “-able” are affixes.
      </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        Affixes can be further categorized into <b>prefixes</b> and
        <b>suffixes</b>, where prefixes always attach to the start of the base,
        while suffixes always attach to the end. In our example, “un-” is a
        prefix, and “-able” is an affix.
      </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        Awesome! You now have a basic understanding of the very core concepts of
        morphology.
      </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        By getting this far, you have unlocked the
        <b>English morphology toolkit</b>, which will allow you to explore the
        morphological breakdown of different English words. <b>Click here</b> to open
        the toolkit.
      </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        Clicking on the “Click here” link/button will turn the website into a
        two-column layout, with this lesson content on the left and the English
        toolkit on the right.
      </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        The user can now experiment with English morphology, or proceed to the
        next section. The toolkit stays visible throughout the rest of the
        experience, unless the user hides it.
      </Paragraph>
    </WaitUntil>
  </Article>
</SectionLayout>
